    "text": "Let\n\\[\nR_k(n)=k-\\frac{k+1}{R_{k+1}(n)}\\qquad (2\\le k<n-1),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nR_{n-1}(n)=(n-1)-\\frac n{-4}=(n-1)+\\frac n4.\n\\]\nThus the continued fraction in the definition of the sequence is \\(1/R_2(n)\\). If \\(R_2(n)=N/D\\) in lowest terms, then \\(1/R_2(n)=D/N\\), so the denominator of the latter, taken positive, is \\(|N|\\). We shall prove that for every prime \\(p\\equiv1\\) or \\(9\\pmod {10}\\), one can choose \\(n\\) so that the reduced numerator of \\(R_2(n)\\) is \\(p\\).\n\n## 1. Integer representatives for the continued-fraction tails\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\). Define integers \\(A_k=A_k(n)\\), for \\(2\\le k\\le n\\), backwards by\n\\[\nA_n=4,\n\\qquad\nA_{n-1}=5n-4,\n\\]\nand, for \\(k+1<n\\),\n\\[\nA_k=kA_{k+1}-(k+1)A_{k+2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThese initial values match the last tail because\n\\[\nR_{n-1}(n)=(n-1)+\\frac n4=\\frac{5n-4}{4}=\\frac{A_{n-1}}{A_n}.\n\\]\nMoreover, whenever the relevant denominators are nonzero, downward induction using (1) gives\n\\[\nR_k(n)=\\frac{A_k}{A_{k+1}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(R_{k+1}=A_{k+1}/A_{k+2}\\), then\n\\[\nR_k=k-\\frac{k+1}{R_{k+1}}\n =k-(k+1)\\frac{A_{k+2}}{A_{k+1}}\n =\\frac{kA_{k+1}-(k+1)A_{k+2}}{A_{k+1}}\n =\\frac{A_k}{A_{k+1}}.\n\\]\nLater we will prove the required nonvanishing for our chosen \\(n\\).\n\nWe next establish a useful identity for these integers. Define integers \\(U_k\\) by\n\\[\nU_2=1,\\qquad U_3=0,\n\\]\nand, for \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\\[\nU_{k+2}=k(U_{k+1}-U_k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nWe claim that for every \\(2\\le k\\le n\\),\n\\[\n(k-1)!A_k=U_kA_2+2(k-2)A_3. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nFor \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\), this follows immediately from \\(U_2=1\\) and \\(U_3=0\\). For larger \\(k\\), rewrite (1), with index \\(k-2\\), as\n\\[\n(k-1)A_k=(k-2)A_{k-1}-A_{k-2}.\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\((k-2)!\\), applying the already established formulas for \\(A_{k-1}\\) and \\(A_{k-2}\\), and using\n\\[\nU_k=(k-2)(U_{k-1}-U_{k-2}),\n\\]\ngives (4). The coefficient of \\(A_3\\) simplifies to \\(2(k-2)\\); this is just the identity\n\\((k-3)-(k-4)=1\\) after the common factorial factors are collected. Thus (4) follows by induction.\n\nA second induction from (3) gives the determinant identity\n\\[\n(n-3)U_n-(n-2)U_{n-1}=-(n-2)! \\qquad(n\\ge3). \\tag{5}\n\\]\nFor \\(n=3\\), its left side is \\(0\\cdot U_3-U_2=-1=-1!\\). If (5) holds at \\(n\\), substitute\n\\[\nU_{n+1}=(n-1)(U_n-U_{n-1})\n\\]\ninto\n\\((n-2)U_{n+1}-(n-1)U_n\\), collect terms, and use\n\\((n-1)!=(n-1)(n-2)!\\); this gives the identity at \\(n+1\\).\n\nWe can now calculate \\(A_2\\). Apply (4) first with \\(k=n\\), and then with \\(k=n-1\\). Since \\(A_n=4\\) and \\(A_{n-1}=5n-4\\), these equations are\n\\[\n4(n-1)!=U_nA_2+2(n-2)A_3, \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n(5n-4)(n-2)!=U_{n-1}A_2+2(n-3)A_3. \\tag{7}\n\\]\nMultiply (6) by \\(n-3\\), multiply (7) by \\(n-2\\), and subtract. The \\(A_3\\)-terms cancel. By (5), the coefficient of \\(A_2\\) is \\(-(n-2)!\\). On the other side, using \\((n-1)!=(n-1)(n-2)!\\), the remaining coefficient is\n\\[\n4(n-3)(n-1)-(n-2)(5n-4)=-(n^2+2n-4).\n\\]\nCancelling the nonzero integer \\((n-2)!\\) therefore yields\n\\[\nA_2=n^2+2n-4. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n## 2. A criterion forcing the sequence value to equal a prescribed prime\n\nWe now prove the following realization criterion.\n\nSuppose \\(p\\) is prime and natural numbers \\(n,q\\) satisfy\n\\[\n3\\le n<p,\\qquad 0<q\\le n-1, \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gcd(q,2(n-2))=1, \\tag{10}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nn^2+2n-4=pq. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThen the \\(n\\)-th sequence value is \\(p\\).\n\nBy (8), \\(A_2=pq\\). Equation (6) becomes\n\\[\n4(n-1)!=U_npq+2(n-2)A_3. \\tag{12}\n\\]\nSince \\(0<q\\le n-1\\), the integer \\(q\\) divides \\((n-1)!\\). It therefore divides both the left side of (12) and the first term on the right, and hence\n\\[\nq\\mid 2(n-2)A_3.\n\\]\nThe coprimality condition (10) now implies\n\\[\nq\\mid A_3. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand,\n\\[\np\\nmid A_3. \\tag{14}\n\\]\nFor if \\(p\\mid A_3\\), then (12) would imply \\(p\\mid4(n-1)!\\). But \\(p>n\\ge3\\), so \\(p\\) is an odd prime, does not divide \\(4\\), and does not divide \\((n-1)!\\), all of whose factors are strictly smaller than \\(p\\). This is impossible.\n\nWe also need to know that no intermediate denominator in (2) vanishes. Suppose, to the contrary, that \\(A_j=0\\) for some \\(3\\le j\\le n\\). Formula (4) at \\(j\\), together with \\(A_2=pq\\), then gives\n\\[\n0=U_jpq+2(j-2)A_3.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(p\\mid2(j-2)A_3\\). By (14) and primality of \\(p\\), this forces \\(p\\mid2\\) or \\(p\\mid j-2\\). The first is impossible because \\(p\\) is odd. The second is also impossible, since\n\\[\n0<j-2<p\n\\]\n(the upper bound follows from \\(j\\le n<p\\)). Thus every \\(A_j\\), \\(3\\le j\\le n\\), is nonzero, and (2) applies all the way down to \\(k=2\\).\n\nBy (13), write \\(A_3=qb\\) for some integer \\(b\\). Equation (14) shows both that \\(b\\ne0\\) and that \\(p\\nmid b\\). We therefore have\n\\[\nR_2(n)=\\frac{A_2}{A_3}=\\frac{pq}{qb}=\\frac pb.\n\\]\nBecause \\(p\\) is prime and does not divide \\(b\\), \\(p\\) and \\(b\\) are coprime. Hence the numerator of this rational number in lowest terms is \\(p\\), up to the sign arising from \\(b\\), and its absolute value is exactly \\(p\\). As observed at the outset, that absolute numerator is the denominator of \\(1/R_2(n)\\). Thus\n\\[\na(n)=p. \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThis proves the criterion.\n\n## 3. Producing parameters from a suitable square root of 5\n\nWe next show how a square root of \\(5\\) modulo \\(p\\) gives parameters satisfying the criterion. Let \\(p\\ge11\\) be prime. Suppose there is an integer \\(r\\) such that\n\\[\n4\\le r,\\qquad 4\\le p-r,\\qquad r^2\\equiv5\\pmod p, \\tag{16}\n\\]\nand such that\n\\[\nq:=\\frac{r^2-5}{p}\n\\]\nis odd. Set\n\\[\nn=r-1. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nThe congruence in (16) and \\(r\\ge4\\) show that \\(p\\mid r^2-5\\), so \\(q\\) is an integer and\n\\[\npq=r^2-5. \\tag{18}\n\\]\nIt is positive because \\(r^2-5>0\\). Also \\(n\\ge3\\), while \\(p-r\\ge4\\) gives \\(n=r-1<p\\).\n\nWe claim that \\(q\\le n-1=r-2\\). From \\(p-r\\ge4\\), we have \\(p\\ge r+4\\). Since \\(r\\ge4\\),\n\\[\nr^2-5\\le(r+4)(r-2),\n\\]\nbecause the right side minus the left side is \\(2r-3>0\\). Therefore\n\\[\npq=r^2-5\\le(r+4)(r-2)\\le p(r-2).\n\\]\nCancelling the positive number \\(p\\) gives \\(q\\le r-2=n-1\\).\n\nIt remains to establish the coprimality required in (10). First we prove\n\\[\n\\gcd(q,r-3)=1. \\tag{19}\n\\]\nLet \\(d\\) divide both \\(q\\) and \\(r-3\\). By (18), \\(d\\mid r^2-5\\). It also divides\n\\[\n(r+3)(r-3)=r^2-9.\n\\]\nSubtracting, we obtain \\(d\\mid4\\). Since \\(q\\) is odd, every divisor \\(d\\) of \\(q\\) is odd. The only positive odd divisor of \\(4\\) is \\(1\\), proving (19). Since \\(q\\) is odd, also \\(\\gcd(q,2)=1\\). As \\(n-2=r-3\\), these two facts imply\n\\[\n\\gcd(q,2(n-2))=\\gcd(q,2(r-3))=1. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nFinally, direct expansion using (17) gives\n\\[\nn^2+2n-4=(r-1)^2+2(r-1)-4=r^2-5=pq. \\tag{21}\n\\]\nThus all hypotheses (9)–(11) hold, and the criterion proves \\(a(n)=p\\).\n\nWe now show that whenever \\(5\\) is a quadratic residue modulo \\(p\\), an \\(r\\) satisfying (16) and the oddness requirement can be chosen. Let \\(a\\), \\(0\\le a<p\\), represent a square root of \\(5\\) modulo \\(p\\):\n\\[\na^2\\equiv5\\pmod p. \\tag{22}\n\\]\nNeither \\(a\\) nor \\(p-a\\) can be less than \\(4\\). Indeed, the possible squares of \\(0,1,2,3\\) are \\(0,1,4,9\\). Since \\(p\\ge11\\), reducing these and \\(5\\) modulo \\(p\\) does not change them, and none equals \\(5\\). Also \\(p-a\\) is the complementary square root because\n\\[\n(p-a)^2\\equiv(-a)^2\\equiv5\\pmod p.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\na\\ge4\\quad\\hbox{and}\\quad p-a\\ge4. \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThe prime \\(p\\ge11\\) is odd. Therefore exactly one of \\(a\\) and \\(p-a\\) is even. Choose the even one and call it \\(r\\). Both choices satisfy (16), by (22)–(23). It remains only to show that\n\\[\n\\frac{r^2-5}{p}\n\\]\nis odd. Since \\(r\\) is even, \\(r^2\\) is even, so \\(r^2-5\\) is odd. More explicitly, if \\(r=2s\\), then\n\\[\nr^2-5=4s^2-5=2(2s^2-3)+1.\n\\]\nThe congruence \\(r^2\\equiv5\\pmod p\\) says that this odd number is \\(p q\\), where \\(q=(r^2-5)/p\\). Since \\(p\\) is odd and a product is odd only when both factors are odd, \\(q\\) is odd. We have therefore produced the required \\(r\\).\n\n## 4. Existence of the square root and conclusion\n\nLet now \\(p\\) be any prime satisfying\n\\[\np\\equiv1\\pmod {10}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p\\equiv9\\pmod {10}. \\tag{24}\n\\]\nThere is no such prime below \\(11\\): among the nonnegative integers below \\(11\\) congruent to \\(1\\) or \\(9\\pmod {10}\\), the only possibilities are \\(1\\) and \\(9\\), neither prime. Hence \\(p\\ge11\\).\n\nReducing (24) modulo \\(5\\), we get\n\\[\np\\equiv1\\pmod5\\quad\\text{or}\\quad p\\equiv4\\pmod5. \\tag{25}\n\\]\nBoth \\(1\\) and \\(4\\) are nonzero squares modulo \\(5\\), namely \\(1^2\\) and \\(2^2\\). Thus \\(p\\) is a quadratic residue modulo \\(5\\).\n\nQuadratic reciprocity now implies that \\(5\\) is a quadratic residue modulo \\(p\\). Indeed, for distinct odd primes \\(p\\) and \\(5\\), quadratic reciprocity states\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac5p\\right)\\left(\\frac p5\\right)\n=(-1)^{((5-1)/2)((p-1)/2)}.\n\\]\nBecause \\((5-1)/2=2\\) is even, the sign on the right is \\(+1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac5p\\right)=\\left(\\frac p5\\right)=1,\n\\]\nthe last equality following from (25). Hence some residue \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a^2\\equiv5\\pmod p\\).\n\nBy the construction in Section 3, choose the even member \\(r\\) of the complementary pair \\(a,p-a\\). It satisfies\n\\[\n4\\le r,\\qquad4\\le p-r,\n\\qquad r^2\\equiv5\\pmod p,\n\\qquad \\frac{r^2-5}{p}\\text{ is odd}.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nn=r-1,\n\\qquad\nq=\\frac{r^2-5}{p}.\n\\]\nSection 3 proves that these numbers satisfy all hypotheses of the realization criterion, and Section 2 therefore gives\n\\[\na(n)=p.\n\\]\nThus every prime congruent to \\(1\\) or \\(9\\pmod {10}\\) occurs in A363347, as required.",
